{"ratings":[{"id":"efe8d1f708c9","clarity":88,"grounding":90,"reasoning":80,"genre_fit":82,"problems":[{"type":"reasoning","quote":"**Every choice of weights gives a negative estimate.** This makes the finding unusually robust.","why":"Invariance to weights is shown only for the point estimate. Section 5's interval (−105 to +25) includes zero and the break-even point, so calling the finding 'unusually robust' overstates the certainty."},{"type":"reasoning","quote":"No plausible value per success turns a negative count of successes into a positive gross value. The sign of the conclusion therefore does not depend on this parameter.","why":"This holds only for the point estimate. Because the interval includes positive counts, the value parameter does affect the expected net value and the risk."},{"type":"reasoning","quote":"Success rates fell in both segments, and the finding holds under any choice of standardization weights.","why":"The recommendation repeats the claim of robustness and omits the statistical uncertainty acknowledged earlier. Neither segment's decline is distinguishable from zero."}]}]}
