{"ratings":[{"id":"dffe3a43389e","clarity":88,"grounding":88,"reasoning":84,"genre_fit":86,"problems":[{"type":"factual","quote":"This is a textbook mix effect (Simpson's-paradox territory).","why":"This is not Simpson's paradox. Both segments and the total move in the same direction; only the size of the total change is inflated by the mix shift."},{"type":"reasoning","quote":"the risk of being wrong is limited","why":"The article's own estimate is about a 25% chance that the true gain falls below break-even, and the interval includes negative effects. Calling the risk limited understates that uncertainty."},{"type":"prose","quote":"The program passes that test, though not by a wide margin.","why":"This sits awkwardly with the later claim that the estimate is more than 2.5 times break-even. The narrow margin is about statistical uncertainty, not the point estimate, and the sentence does not say so."}]}]}
